  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Wed May 13 2026\n\nIn the near term: A low stratus deck has expanded into eastern/ \nsouthern portions of the area, and this is disrupting the fog \nformation process. Will monitor the trends, and we may be able to\ndrop the limited dense fog graphic if the stratus proves to be \npersistent. Any stratus will dissipate later this morning, and \nthis will be followed by sunny skies as we go into the afternoon. \nA weak, dry cold front will then cross the forecast area late \ntoday into tonight bringing little to no weather impacts to the\nforecast area. \n\nGoing through the remainder of the week and weekend, we'll see a\na weather pattern change with the mean subtropical ridge axis\nshifting from the Southern Plains to the south Atlantic coast. \nWhile it will remain dry, the resulting shift from northerly to \nsoutherly low level winds will increase the humidity quite a bit, \nand temperatures will be warming up with highs reaching the lower \n90s by Monday over northern portions of the area. Looking ahead \nto mid/late next week, we should eventually see rain chances\nmake an increase. /EC/",
